  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nIt is a chilly afternoon with most locations still below\nfreezing for temperatures. Only exception is across parts of \nwestern Wisconsin where this morning's cloud cover lingered \nlonger. Though even those spots are still in the low 30s. Clouds\nwill move in again tonight from the southwest, though not until\nafter midnight. This will allow for several hours of efficient \nradiational cooling, dropping everywhere into the teens, if not \nthe single digits in some of the more low-lying spots.\n\nA slight warm-up starting Tuesday will bring our highs into the\nmid- upper 30s by Wednesday. At the same time, an area of weak\nforcing will slide across the region and likely produce light \nsnow near the Dakotas/MN border Tuesday night into Wednesday. \nOur latest PoPs max out around 30-50% do to low confidence in \nthe areal coverage of any snow showers. Given the timing of it, \nwe would not be surprised to have some light (a tenth or two) \naccumulation on grassy surfaces, otherwise any impacts from this\nwave are expected to be minimal. \n\nTemperatures continue to warm-up slightly as we head into the  \nholiday weekend. The upper level pattern is a little chaotic as\nwe have a Clipper low move southeast towards the Great Lakes \nat the same time as a second trough deepens over the Arklatex \nregion. The collision is expected to form an organized low that \ntravels from Illinois and across the Ohio Valley. Even with an \nexpansive precip shield, the bulk of the action is expected to \nstay well to our southeast. As folks travel for the holiday, \nthis will be important to look more into across that area.\n\nAnother wave is expected later in the weekend (Fri/Sat most\nlikely) that could bring a chance for light rain or snow. \nThere is also no strong signal though, with weaker forcing and \nlimited moisture available behind the larger system on \nThursday. Overall, there will be several small chances for areas\nto see their first snowfall throughout the end of the month, \nhowever it will not be surprising if many go through the whole \nmonth without seeing a single flake given the weak nature of \neach system for our area.",
